NOTES AND QUESTIONS ON CURTIS V. LOETHER
1. Who wanted a trial by jury? Why?

2. The notion that a “jury of one’s peers” serves as a bulwark against judicial caprice, bias, or heavy-handedness is deeply engrained in American legal culture. When a would-be renter sues a landlord, in what sense and in what circumstances might we expect the jury to be “peers” of the renter, or of the landlord? Should this matter to the Seventh Amendment analysis?

